                "text": "**Analyzing Rectangle Properties**\n\nMy focus has shifted to detailing the visual characteristics of these distinct solid rectangles. I'm specifically observing how the external border, a singular pixel width, retains the primary color of the shape, while the interior, presumably the space within that border, is also a consideration for how it's represented or defined.\n\n**Drawing Diagonal Patterns**\n\nI've observed that the interior of the rectangles isn't uniformly colored but instead features an \"X\" or hourglass shape. This diagonal pattern appears to connect the inner corners of the rectangle and is drawn using the color of the inner dot.\n\n**Refining Interior Pattern Logic**\n\nI've encountered an anomaly in one rectangle where the diagonal lines seem to connect and form a horizontal segment in the center. This suggests my current \"X\" pattern model might need an update to account for these merged diagonal elements, potentially revealing a skeleton-like structure.\n\n**Observing Dimensional Interactions**\n\nI've confirmed that the intersection point or segment of the interior diagonal pattern directly correlates with the relative dimensions of the rectangle's interior. When width is less than height, a vertical segment forms; when width exceeds height, a horizontal segment appears. For squares, they meet at a single point.\n\n**Refining Diagonal Intersection Logic**\n\nI'm noticing a pattern in the interior where the diagonals merge. Specifically, when the height is less than the width, and a cell's vertical distance from the nearest horizontal edge matches the maximum possible vertical distance within the inner rectangle, it seems to contribute to a horizontal segment. This is in addition to the standard diagonal checks.\n\n**Identifying Skeleton Formation**\n\nI've realized the interior pattern is a direct representation of the rectangle's skeleton. It forms where the distances to opposite edges are tied and maximal, or where a distance to an edge reaches its maximum possible value within the inner space.\n\n**Confirming Skeleton Formation**\n\nI've observed that the interior patterns indeed represent the rectangle's skeleton. The logic appears to be solidifying around distances to edges and their relation to maximum values within the inner dimensions, correctly generating the expected visual structures for both squares and varied aspect ratios.\n\n**Verifying Rectangle Boundaries**\n\nI'm meticulously re-examining the precise row and column boundaries for rectangles of colors 2 and 3. My recent checks confirm the 8x8 inner dimensions for color 2 and the 4x4 inner space for color 3, aligning perfectly with visual observations. I'm now scrutinizing the start and end columns for color 8.\n\n",
